# Git workflow

- At most one open agent PR per repo at a time. When it is still open, add
  new changes and fixes to its branch; open a new PR only after it merges.
  Dependabot and the Changesets version PR are the only exceptions. Many
  open PRs make it impossible to keep an overview. Branch from `main`,
  commit as you go, and push once `pnpm check` passes. CI runs the full `pnpm verify`; watch it
  with `pnpm ci:wait` and fix what it reports in a follow-up commit.
- Commit messages follow Conventional Commits (`commitlint.config.ts`) with
  body lines of at most 100; the header has no length limit. Group
  commits by track (`ci:`, `build:`, `docs:`), not by file.
- Lefthook runs `ultracite fix` and `pnpm check` (lint, typecheck and
  mirrored tests of the changed workspaces) on commit and push, and commitlint
  on the message.
  Tests run in CI, so run the affected workspace's tests before you push a
  code change. Fix what a hook reports instead of skipping it.
- A user-visible change needs a changeset (`pnpm changeset`). Private
  workspaces and repo tooling don't; say so in the commit body.
- Never commit `.cursor/hooks/state/*`; it is local hook state.
- Never force-push `main`, and never rewrite a pushed migration.
- The PR description says what changed, how it was verified, and any manual
  step a maintainer has to take (a dashboard setting, a required check).
